==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*brūkaną||to enjoy, use}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰrūg-||enjoy}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|bruka|brūka|lang=ofs}}, Old High German {{term|bruhhan|brūhhan|lang=goh}} (German [[brauchen]]), Old Saxon {{term|brukan|brūkan|lang=osx}} (Dutch [[gebruiken]]), Gothic {{term|𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽|tr=brūkjan|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin [[frui]] ‘enjoy’ and [[frux]] ‘fruit’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbruːkɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=II|head=brūcan}}

# to [[enjoy]], to make use of
# to [[digest]] food

